[Chapter "Key ideas: Why the Queen's Gambit"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Why play the Queen's Gambit? After 1.d4 d5, White can't play e4 yet, so 2.c4 attacks Black's center pawn from the side. If Black takes on c4, White builds a full center and wins the pawn back. If Black holds d5 with ...e6 or ...c6, White's pieces come out to natural squares, and the pawn on d4 is safe because the queen guards it. There are few early tactics against White's king.

Our repertoire is built around one pawn structure. Against the Queen's Gambit Declined (2...e6) we play the Exchange Variation: take on d5 at once, then play the minority attack, a plan that works without long theory. Against the Slav (2...c6), the main line 4...e6 leads to the same structure. Against the Queen's Gambit Accepted (2...dxc4), 3.e3 wins the pawn back with Bxc4 and sets one of the oldest traps in chess.

Black answers 2.c4 with 2...e6 in a third of your games, 2...c6 in a quarter, 2...dxc4 in about one game in six and 2...Nf6 in about one in nine.

It is one of the oldest openings: it already appears in the Göttingen manuscript, a chess book written more than 500 years ago. Nearly every game of the 1927 world championship match between José Raúl Capablanca and Alexander Alekhine began with the Queen's Gambit Declined.

Step through the moves to see the logic.}
1. d4 {Takes the center. The queen guards the pawn from d1.} d5 {[%cal Rd5e4] Black's pawn stops e4 for now.}
2. c4 {[%cal Gc4d5] The Queen's Gambit. A wing pawn attacks d5, Black's only center pawn.

It isn't a real gambit. After 2...dxc4 3.e3, White wins the pawn back with Bxc4, and Black gets into trouble trying to keep it (see Typical tactics).} e6 {[%cal Re6d5] [%csl Yc8] The Queen's Gambit Declined. Black holds d5 with a pawn and keeps a solid center. The cost is the c8 bishop, shut in behind the pawn on e6.

Black's main alternatives: 2...c6, the Slav, keeps the bishop free but takes c6 away from the knight. 2...dxc4, the Accepted, gives up the center for quick development.}
3. Nc3 {A second attacker on d5.} Nf6 {Black guards d5 again and stops e4. Black plays this in six games out of ten.}
4. cxd5 {[%cal Gc1g5] The Exchange Variation. White takes before Black can take on c4, and fixes the pawn structure for the rest of the game.

Black takes back with the pawn in three games out of four. 4...Nxd5 gives White two center pawns after 5.e4; Black's best is 5...Nxc3 6.bxc3, and White keeps the bigger center (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).} exd5 {The usual recapture. It opens the diagonal of Black's c8 bishop, so the problem piece is free again. White's next moves, Bg5, Qc2 and Bd3, keep it from finding a good square.}
5. Bg5 {[%cal Gg5f6,Rf6d5] Pins the knight that guards d5. The plans that follow are on the next page.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Plans for both sides"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "r1bqrnk1/pp2bppp/2p2n2/3p2B1/3P4/2NBP3/PPQ1NPPP/R4RK1 w - - 7 11"]

{[%cal Ga1b1,Gb2b4,Gb4b5,Gf2f3,Ge3e4,Rf6e4,Rf8g6,Re7d6] [%csl Yc6,Ye4] This is the Exchange Variation after 5.Bg5 c6 6.Qc2 Be7 7.e3 Nbd7 8.Bd3 O-O 9.Nge2 Re8 10.O-O Nf8. The green arrows are White's plans, the red arrows Black's. Stockfish rates it as equal (+0.2): the chances are balanced, but White's plans are easier to play.

Plan 1: the minority attack. White's two queenside pawns (a and b) attack Black's three (a, b and c). Put a rook on b1, then push b4 and b5. When White takes on c6, Black is left with a weak pawn: a backward pawn on c6, on the half-open c-file, or an isolated pawn on d5 after ...cxb5. White's rooks and knights then attack it.

Plan 2: f3 and e4. The knight on e2, not f3, leaves the f-pawn free. f3 takes the e4 and g4 squares away from Black's pieces and prepares e4, which opens the center while White's pieces are ready.

Black's plans. Black has more room on the kingside and uses the e4 square: ...Ne4 trades a pair of minor pieces, and ...Ng6 and ...Bd6 aim at White's king. Black also wants to trade the c8 bishop, with ...Be6 or ...Bg4, or with ...Bf5 after ...g6. White's queen on c2 and bishop on d3 control f5, which is why we play Qc2 early.

The Slav. After 3.Nf3 Nf6 4.Nc3 e6 5.cxd5 exd5 6.Bg5 the pawn structure is the same, and so are the plans (Stockfish −0.1, equal). Against 4...dxc4, play 5.a4 Bf5 6.e3 e6 7.Bxc4: White wins the pawn back, castles, and prepares e4 with Qe2 and Rd1 (Stockfish +0.2, equal, after 7...Bb4 8.O-O O-O 9.Qe2).

The Accepted. After 3.e3 and Bxc4, White also aims for e4. If Black hits the center with ...c5 and ...cxd4, White takes back with the e-pawn and gets an isolated pawn on d4 with active pieces (next page).} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Pawn structures"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pp3ppp/4pn2/8/2BP4/5N2/PP3PPP/RNBQ1RK1 b kq - 0 7"]

{[%cal Gf3e5,Gc1g5,Gd4d5,Rd8d4,Rb8c6] [%csl Yd4] Three pawn structures come up again and again in this course.

The Carlsbad structure: the Exchange Variation, and the Slav with 4...e6. White's pawns on d4 and e3 face Black's pawns on d5 and c6, and the c- and e-pawns have been traded. It is named after the 1923 Carlsbad tournament. White has the half-open c-file, Black the half-open e-file. White's queenside pawns are a minority, two against three, but they are the ones that create the weakness: b4, b5 and bxc6 leave a backward pawn on c6 or an isolated pawn on d5. Black's answer is piece play on the kingside and in the center, with a knight on e4.

The isolated queen's pawn: this position, from the Accepted after 3.e3 Nf6 4.Bxc4 e6 5.Nf3 c5 6.O-O cxd4 7.exd4. White's d-pawn has no pawn neighbors, so pieces must guard it. In return White gets open lines and the e5 square for a knight. Keep pieces on the board: develop with Nc3, Re1 and Bg5 or Bf4, put a knight on e5, and look for an attack on the kingside or the break d5. If many pieces are traded, the pawn becomes a target in the endgame. Stockfish rates this position +0.4, slightly better for White.

The Slav center, after 4...dxc4 5.a4. Black's pawns on c6 and e6 hold d5, and Black has taken a pawn on c4. White wins it back with e3 and Bxc4, and a4 stops ...b5. Then White plays for e4, with Qe2 and Rd1. Once the pawn reaches e4, White has the bigger center.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Typical tactics"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/p3pppp/8/1p6/2pP4/4P3/1P3PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 6"]

{[%cal Gd1f3,Gf3a8] [%csl Ra8] Six patterns to know.

The Qf3 trap. This position comes from the Accepted after 3.e3 b5? 4.a4 c6 5.axb5 cxb5?. Black has kept the extra pawn, but the long diagonal is open: 6.Qf3 hits the rook on a8, and Black can't save it without losing a piece. The best try is 6...Nc6 7.Qxc6+ Bd7, and White is a piece up (Stockfish +5.7, winning for White). Black tries to keep the pawn with 3...b5 in almost one game in five, so expect this position.

The rook on a8. In the same line, 4...a6? doesn't hold the pawn either. After 5.axb5, 5...axb5?? 6.Rxa8 wins the rook, because the a-file is open (Stockfish +7.0, winning for White).

Qb3 against ...Bg4. When Black's light-squared bishop leaves c8, b7 is loose, and a queen on b3 hits b7 and f7 together. After 3.e3 Nf6 4.Bxc4 Nc6 5.Nf3 Bg4 6.Qb3, Black loses a pawn (Stockfish +1.9, clearly better for White), and 6...Bxf3? 7.Bxf7+ Kd7 8.Qe6# is checkmate.

Nxd5 when c8 is loose. In the Exchange, after 5.Bg5 c6 6.Qc2 Be7 7.e3, the move 7...Nh5? looks like a good way to trade White's bishop. But after 8.Bxe7 Qxe7, 9.Nxd5! wins a pawn. If 9...cxd5?, the c-file is open and 10.Qxc8+ takes the bishop with check (Stockfish +5.6, winning for White). After 9...Qd6, Black is just a pawn down (Stockfish +3.1, winning for White). The trick works only while Black's knight is on b8, because then nothing guards c8.

Don't take on d5: the Elephant trap. With Black's knight on d7, the pawn on d5 looks free after 4.cxd5 exd5 5.Bg5 Nbd7, because the knight on f6 is pinned. But 6.Nxd5?? Nxd5! 7.Bxd8 Bb4+ 8.Qd2 Bxd2+ 9.Kxd2 Kxd8 leaves Black a piece up (Stockfish −4.9, winning for Black). Play 6.e3 and develop.

Qb3 in the Slav. After 3.Nf3 Nf6 4.Nc3 Bf5?! 5.cxd5 cxd5, Black's bishop has left, and nothing guards b7. 6.Qb3 hits it. Black should go back with 6...Bc8 (Stockfish +0.5, slightly better for White). The natural 6...Qd7? runs into 7.e4!, and White's knight comes to e5 with gain of time (Stockfish +4.2, winning for White).} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Gambits and early surprises"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Not every opponent plays 2...e6, 2...c6 or 2...dxc4. The rarer second moves try to surprise you, but each has a simple answer. The rule: when Black gives up the center or offers a pawn, take what is offered and develop.

2...Nf6, the Marshall Defense, comes up in about one game in nine. Play 3.cxd5! Black can't take back with a pawn. After 3...Nxd5 4.Nf3, e4 follows soon and gains time on the knight (Stockfish +1.1, clearly better for White). After 3...Qxd5 4.Nc3, the queen is chased again (Stockfish +1.2, clearly better for White).

2...Nc6, the Chigorin Defense: 3.Nf3, then Nc3 and Bg5 (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White).

2...Bf5, the Baltic Defense: 3.cxd5. After 3...Bxb1 4.Rxb1 Qxd5 5.e3, Black has given up the bishop pair (Stockfish +1.0, clearly better for White).

2...c5, the Austrian Defense: 3.cxd5, and White is a little better whichever way Black takes back (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White).

2...e5, the Albin Countergambit, about one game in twenty, is the most dangerous. Step through the moves.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e5 {[%cal Re5d4] Black offers a pawn for quick development and a cramping pawn on d4.}
3. dxe5 {Take it.} d4 {[%csl Yd4] The pawn on d4 takes c3 away from White's knight. Black wants ...Nc6, ...Bg4 or ...Be6, and often castles queenside.}
4. Nf3 {The right move: it develops and guards e5.

Avoid 4.e3?, which looks natural but walks into the Lasker trap. After 4...Bb4+ 5.Bd2 dxe3!, the greedy 6.Bxb4? exf2+ 7.Ke2 fxg1=N+! promotes to a knight with check. After 8.Rxg1 Bg4+, Black wins White's queen (Stockfish −9.8, winning for Black). Even the best defense, 6.fxe3, leaves White with nothing (Stockfish −0.1, equal).} Nc6 {Black's usual move: it attacks e5.}
5. a3 {[%cal Gb2b4,Gc1b2] Stops ...Bb4+ and prepares b4 and Bb2, aimed at the pawn on d4. White keeps the extra pawn (Stockfish +1.0, clearly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Exchange: 5...Be7"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation, Reshevsky Variation"]
[ECO "D36"]

{The main line of the course. After 4.cxd5 exd5 5.Bg5, Black's most common reply is 5...Be7, in half of these games. White develops with e3, Bd3 and Qc2, brings the king's knight to e2 and castles short. The knight on e2 leaves the f-pawn free, so this chapter shows the f3 and e4 plan. The next chapter shows the minority attack.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 Be7 {Breaks the pin, so the knight on f6 can move again.}
6. e3 {Opens the diagonal of the f1 bishop.

6...Bf5?! develops the bishop, but it leaves b7 without a guard. 7.Bxf6 Bxf6 8.Qb3 hits b7 and d5 together. After 8...c6 9.Qxb7 Qb6 10.Qxb6 axb6, White is a pawn up in the endgame (Stockfish +1.0, clearly better for White).} O-O
7. Bd3 {[%cal Gd3h7,Gd3f5] The bishop aims at h7 and covers f5, the best square for Black's c8 bishop.

Against 7...c5, play 8.Nge2. After 8...cxd4 9.Nxd4, Black is left with an isolated pawn on d5 (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).} h6 {Asks the bishop to decide. Against Black's other seventh moves, keep developing with Qc2, Nge2 and castling.}
8. Bh4 {Keeps the pin. The bishop can drop back to g3 later.} c6 {Holds d5 and gives the queen a way out to b6 or a5.}
9. Qc2 {[%cal Gc2h7] This is the Reshevsky Variation. The queen joins the bishop on the diagonal to h7 and keeps Black's bishop off f5.

9...Be6 is the other main move. Play 10.Nge2 and the same plan: castle, then f3 and e4 (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).} Nbd7
10. Nge2 {[%cal Gf2f3] The knight goes to e2, not f3, so the f-pawn can move.} Re8 11. O-O Nf8 {[%cal Gf8g6,Gf8e6] The knight guards h7 and heads for g6 or e6.}
12. f3 {[%cal Ge3e4] Takes e4 and g4 away from Black's knights and prepares e4. Bring a rook to e1 or d1 first, then push e4 when the pieces are ready. The game is level, but White's plan is clear and Black has to find active moves. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Exchange: 5...c6"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation, Reshevsky Variation"]
[ECO "D36"]

{Black's second most common reply, in about a quarter of these games. Black guards d5 first and keeps the f8 bishop's choice open. Play 6.Qc2 at once: it stops ...Bf5, Black's most natural way to free the c8 bishop. Then e3, Bd3, Nge2 and castle, and start the minority attack with Rab1 and b4.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 c6
6. Qc2 {[%cal Gc2f5] This is the Reshevsky Variation. The queen covers f5, so the c8 bishop can't come out there yet.

Black can prepare ...Bf5 with ...g6. After 6...g6 7.e3 Bf5 8.Bd3 Bxd3 9.Qxd3, or 6...h6 7.Bh4 g6 8.e3 Bf5 9.Bd3 Bxd3 10.Qxd3, the light-squared bishops are traded and the game is level (Stockfish 0.0 and +0.1, equal). Against 6...Bd6, play 7.e3 and develop as usual (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).} Be7
7. e3 {7...Nh5? looks like a good way to trade off White's bishop, but it leaves c8 without a guard: see the trap chapter on 7...Nh5.} O-O 8. Bd3 Nbd7 9. Nge2 Re8 10. O-O Nf8
11. Rab1 {[%cal Gb2b4,Gb4b5] The minority attack begins. Next b4 and b5, then bxc6, to leave Black with a weak pawn on c6 or d5. Black's chances lie on the kingside, with ...Ne4 or ...Ng6. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Exchange: 4...Nxd5"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Semi-Tarrasch Defense, Exchange Variation"]
[ECO "D41"]

{About one player in four takes back on d5 with the knight. Black keeps a flexible pawn structure, but the knight can be chased. Answer with 5.e4: White gains time and builds a full center. Black's best is to trade knights and hit the center with ...c5 at once.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 Nxd5
5. e4 {[%cal Ge4d5] Hits the knight and takes the center.

If the knight retreats, with 5...Nf6 or 5...Nb6, then 6.Nf3 gives White a big center for free (Stockfish +1.2, clearly better for White). 5...Bb4?? loses a piece to 6.exd5 (Stockfish +6.4, winning for White).} Nxc3
6. bxc3 {[%csl Yc3,Yd4,Ye4] White's pawns on c3, d4 and e4 form a strong center, and the b-file is half-open for a rook.} c5 {[%cal Rc5d4] The right way: Black hits d4 at once. 6...Be7 is slower, and White keeps a bigger edge after 7.Nf3 (Stockfish +0.7, slightly better for White).}
7. Nf3 {This is the Semi-Tarrasch Defense, Exchange Variation. The knight guards d4.

7...Nc6? is a mistake: 8.d5! drives the knight away, and White's pawns roll forward (Stockfish +2.2, winning for White).} cxd4 8. cxd4 Bb4+ 9. Bd2 Bxd2+ {Trading pieces eases Black's defense, because Black has less space.} 10. Qxd2 O-O 11. Bc4 {[%cal Gc4f7] The bishop takes an active diagonal, aimed at f7.} b6 12. O-O Bb7 13. Rfe1 {[%cal Gd4d5,Ge1e4] White has the bigger center, and d5 is the break: when the pawn advances, lines open toward Black's king. Black wants to blockade with pieces and attack d4 with ...Nc6 and ...Rc8. Stockfish: +0.4, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Slav: 4...e6"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation, Reshevsky Variation"]
[ECO "D36"]

{The Slav (2...c6) comes up in a quarter of your games. Play 3.Nf3 and 4.Nc3. Against 4...e6, the most common reply, take on d5. After 5.cxd5 exd5 6.Bg5 the pawns are the same as in the Exchange Variation, with Black's pawn already on c6 and White's knight on f3. The plans are the same too: Bd3, Qc2, castle, and the minority attack.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 {The Slav. The c-pawn guards d5 and keeps the c8 bishop's diagonal open.}
3. Nf3 {Develops and guards d4.} Nf6 4. Nc3 {This is the Three Knights Variation.} e6 {The Semi-Slav. Black shuts in the c8 bishop again, but d5 is very solid.}
5. cxd5 {[%cal Gc4d5] Fixes the pawns and steers the game into a structure you already know.

5...cxd5, about three games in ten, is under Advanced variations. 5...Nxd5 gives White the center after 6.e4 (Stockfish +1.1, clearly better for White).} exd5
6. Bg5 {[%cal Gg5f6] The same pin as in the Exchange Variation. Against 6...h6, keep the pin with 7.Bh4. After 7...Bf5 8.e3, develop with Bd3 and trade bishops (Stockfish −0.2, equal).} Be7
7. e3 {7...Bf5 can be met by 8.Nh4, which chases the bishop and gets ready to take it (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Against 7...Bg4, play 8.Qc2 and Bd3 (Stockfish +0.2, equal).} O-O 8. Bd3 Nbd7
9. Qc2 {This is the Reshevsky Variation, reached by another move order.} Re8 10. O-O Nf8
11. Rab1 {[%cal Gb2b4,Gb4b5] The same minority attack as in the Exchange: b4, b5 and bxc6. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Slav: 4...Bf5"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Three Knights Variation"]
[ECO "D15"]

{About one Slav player in five develops the bishop at once with 4...Bf5. Black frees the bishop before playing ...e6, but b7 loses its guard. Take on d5 and play 6.Qb3: the queen hits b7, and d5 needs watching too. Many club players go wrong here (see the trap chapter on ...b6? and ...Qd7?). Against the best defenses, White keeps a small, lasting edge.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 {This is the Three Knights Variation.} Bf5 {[%csl Yb7] The bishop comes out before ...e6, but nothing guards b7 now.}
5. cxd5 {Opens the c-file, and the queen can reach b3.

About one player in four takes back with the knight. 6.Qb3 hits b7 again, and after 6...Nxc3 7.bxc3 Qb6 8.Ne5, White is slightly better (Stockfish +0.6).} cxd5
6. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7,Gb3d5] Hits b7 and adds pressure on d5.

Black's best is 6...Bc8, which goes back to guard b7. Develop with 7.Bg5 e6 8.e3: Black has lost time, and the c8 bishop is shut in again (Stockfish +0.5, slightly better for White).

6...e6? lets White take a pawn: 7.Qxb7 Nbd7 8.Bf4 (Stockfish +1.5, clearly better for White). 6...b6? and 6...Qd7? run into 7.e4!: see the trap chapter.} Qb6 {Offers a queen trade to cover b7.}
7. Qxb6 {Trades queens and leaves Black with doubled pawns.} axb6 {[%csl Yb6,Yb7] Black's b-pawns are doubled, but the a-file is open for the rook.}
8. e3 {[%cal Gf3e5,Gc3b5] Next Ne5, Bd2 and Nb5, aimed at c7 and the doubled pawns. White has an easy endgame plan. Stockfish: +0.5, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Accepted: 3...Nf6"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted: Furman Variation"]
[ECO "D27"]

{The Queen's Gambit Accepted (2...dxc4) comes up in about one game in six. 3.e3 opens the diagonal of the f1 bishop, and Bxc4 wins the pawn back at once. Against Black's quiet development, ...Nf6, ...e6 and ...c5, White castles and chooses between two simple plans: trade queens on d8 for a small, safe edge in the endgame, or keep the queens and push e4.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 {The Queen's Gambit Accepted. Black gives up the center for a moment to free the c8 bishop.}
3. e3 {[%cal Gf1c4] Opens the diagonal of the f1 bishop. Bxc4 comes next, and the pawn is back.} Nf6 {The most common reply. 3...b5 tries to keep the pawn: see the trap chapter on 3...b5.}
(3... e6 4. Bxc4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {The same position as in the main line after 5.Nf3. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.})
4. Bxc4 e6 5. Nf3 c5 {[%cal Rc5d4] Black hits d4 at once, the main freeing move of the Accepted.}
(5... Be7 {Slower. White uses the time to prepare e4.} 6. O-O O-O 7. Qe2 {[%cal Ge3e4] Prepares e4 and frees d1 for the rook.} a6 8. e4 {White has the big center.} b5 9. Bd3 {[%cal Ge4e5] Next e5 drives the knight away from f6, Black's best defender of the king. Stockfish: +0.6, slightly better for White.})
6. O-O {Castle first. Against 6...cxd4, take back with the pawn: 7.exd4 gives White an isolated pawn on d4 and active pieces (see Key ideas: Pawn structures).} a6 {[%cal Rb7b5] Prepares ...b5, which would gain time on the bishop.}
7. dxc5 {Takes the pawn before ...b5 can come with tempo.} Bxc5 {This is the Furman Variation.}
8. Qxd8+ {Trades queens. Black's king can't castle any more and stays in the center.} Kxd8 9. Be2 {The bishop steps back before ...b5 hits it.} Ke7 10. Nc3 b5 11. Nd2 {[%cal Gd2b3,Gb3c5] The knight heads for b3, where it hits the bishop on c5. White's pieces are a little more active. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Accepted: 3...e5"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted: Old Variation"]
[ECO "D20"]

{Black hits back in the center at once. After 4.Bxc4 exd4 5.exd4, White has an isolated pawn on d4, but quick development and open lines for the pieces. Develop with Nf3 and Nc3, castle, and use the open e-file.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 e5 {[%cal Re5d4] Black strikes at the center before White has won the pawn back.}
4. Bxc4 {Wins the pawn back first. The e5 pawn isn't going anywhere.} exd4
5. exd4 {[%csl Yd4] Now the d-pawn is isolated, but the c1 bishop's diagonal is open, and the e-file too. Against 5...Bb4+, block with 6.Nc3 and develop (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).} Nf6
6. Nf3 {6...Bd6 is Black's best. After 7.Qe2+ Qe7 8.Qxe7+ Bxe7, the queens are off and the game is level (Stockfish +0.2, equal).} Bb4+ 7. Nc3 {Develops and blocks the check.} O-O
8. O-O {[%cal Gf1e1,Gc1g5] White is ready for h3, Bg5 or Bf4, and Re1. The pawn on d4 gives White space and the e5 square. Stockfish: +0.5, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Marshall: 3...Nxd5"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Marshall Defense"]
[ECO "D06"]

{2...Nf6 comes up in about one game in nine. Take on d5 at once: Black can't take back with a pawn, so White gets the center. After 3...Nxd5, play 4.Nf3 and 5.e4, which gains time on the knight. White is clearly better in every line of this chapter.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 Nf6 {This is the Marshall Defense. Black develops, but now only the queen guards d5.}
3. cxd5 {Takes the d-pawn. Black has to take back with a piece.} Nxd5 {3...Qxd5 brings the queen out early: see Advanced variations.}
4. Nf3 {Develops and prepares e4. Black's other fourth moves, 4...Bg4, 4...Bf5 and 4...g6, are under Advanced variations.} e6 {Opens the diagonal of the f8 bishop.}
(4... Nc6 {Hits d4.} 5. e4 {Chases the knight.} Nb6 {5...Nf6 6.Nc3 Bg4 7.d5 Ne5? walks into a trap: see the trap chapter on 7...Ne5.} 6. d5 {Gains time on the other knight.} Nb8 7. a4 {[%cal Ga4a5] Threatens a5, which drives the b6 knight back too. White has the center, and Black's pieces are back home. Stockfish: +1.9, clearly better for White.})
5. e4 {Builds the full center with gain of time.} Nf6 {After 5...Nb6 or 5...Bb4+, White develops with 6.Nc3 or 6.Bd2 and keeps a big center (Stockfish +1.2 to +1.4, clearly better for White).}
6. Nc3 Bb4 {[%cal Rb4c3,Rf6e4] Pins the knight and hits e4.}
7. Bd3 {Guards e4. Against 7...c5, play 8.a3: the bishop has to decide (Stockfish +1.8, clearly better for White). Watch for 7...O-O??, which loses to 8.e5 and a bishop sacrifice on h7: see the trap chapter on the Greek gift.} Nc6
8. O-O {[%cal Ge4e5,Gd4d5] White has the center and the threats e5 and d5. Black has no good way to hit back. Stockfish: +2.4, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Albin: 4...Nc6"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Albin Countergambit, Normal Line"]
[ECO "D08"]

{The Albin Countergambit (2...e5) comes up in about one game in twenty, and it is full of traps for a careless White. The safe recipe: take the pawn with 3.dxe5, develop with 4.Nf3, then play 5.a3 to stop ...Bb4+ and prepare b4. Later White gives the pawn back on good terms and keeps the bishop pair against Black's loose pawns.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e5 {This is the Albin Countergambit. Black offers a pawn for quick development and a cramping pawn on d4.}
3. dxe5 d4 {[%csl Yd4] The pawn takes c3 and e3 away from White's pieces.}
4. Nf3 {Develops and guards e5.}
(4. e3? {Looks natural, but walks into a famous trap.} Bb4+ 5. Bd2 dxe3 {This is the Lasker Trap.} 6. Bxb4? {6.fxe3 is the only move, and then White has nothing (Stockfish −0.1, equal).} exf2+ 7. Ke2 fxg1=N+! {Promotes to a knight with check. A new queen would be taken by the rook at once.} 8. Rxg1 Bg4+ {The bishop check wins White's queen on d1. Stockfish: −9.8, winning for Black.})
4... Nc6 {Black's usual move: it hits e5.}
5. a3 {[%cal Gb2b4,Gc1b2] Stops ...Bb4+ and prepares b4 and Bb2, which attack d4.

5...Nge7 heads for g6 to win e5 back. Play 6.b4 Ng6 7.Bb2: White's pieces hit d4, and if Black takes on e5, the d4 pawn falls (Stockfish +0.7, slightly better for White).} Bg4 {[%cal Rg4f3] Pins the knight on f3, which guards e5.}
6. Nbd2 {Guards the knight on f3, so ...Bxf3 can be met by a knight.} Nge7 {The most common reply. The knight heads for g6 to win e5 back.}
7. h3 {Asks the bishop to decide.} Bxf3 8. Nxf3 {Now White has the bishop pair.} Ng6 {Hits e5 twice.}
9. e6! {Gives the pawn back on White's terms. After 9...fxe6, Black's pawns on e6 and d4 are loose.} fxe6
10. g3 {[%cal Gf1g2,Gg2b7] The bishop goes to g2, aimed at b7 and the center. White has two bishops, and Black's pawns on d4 and e6 are targets. Stockfish: +1.4, clearly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Exchange: 5...Nbd7 and 5...Bb4"]
[Diagram "5...Nbd7"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation, Reshevsky Variation"]
[ECO "D36"]

{Two rarer fifth moves, each in about one Exchange game in twelve. 5...Nbd7 sets the Elephant trap: don't grab d5, and develop as usual with e3, Bd3, Qc2 and Nge2. 5...Bb4 pins the knight on c3: castle quickly and keep d4 well guarded. 5...Be6, in about one game in twenty, is met the same way: 6.e3, then Bd3 and Nge2 (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 Nbd7 {[%csl Yd5] The pawn on d5 looks loose, because the knight on f6 is pinned.}
(5... Bb4 {Pins the knight on c3.} 6. e3 {Against 6...h6, keep the pin with 7.Bh4 and develop the same way.} O-O 7. Bd3 {Castle next, and keep d4 well guarded. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.})
6. e3 {Develop. With the knight on d7, taking on d5 loses a piece.}
(6. Nxd5?? {The Elephant trap. White wins a pawn but loses a piece.} Nxd5! 7. Bxd8 Bb4+ 8. Qd2 Bxd2+ 9. Kxd2 Kxd8 {Black has won a knight for a pawn. Stockfish: −4.9, winning for Black.})
6... c6 {The most common reply. After 6...Be7, 7.Bd3 c6 8.Qc2 reaches the same position.}
7. Bd3 Be7 8. Qc2 {This is the Reshevsky Variation.} O-O 9. Nge2 Re8 10. O-O Nf8 {The same position as in Exchange: 5...c6 after 10...Nf8. There we played the minority attack; here is the other plan.}
11. f3 {[%cal Ge3e4] Takes e4 away from Black's knights and prepares e4. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "QGD: 3...c6"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Semi-Slav Defense: Accelerated Move Order"]
[ECO "D31"]

{About one Black player in nine plays 3...c6, keeping the choice between Semi-Slav and Slav setups open. Take on d5 anyway. Without a knight on f6 there is nothing to pin, so the bishop goes to f4 instead of g5. 3...dxc4 is rarer: 4.e4 takes the center, and after 4...Bb4 5.Bxc4 White is slightly better (Stockfish +0.7).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 c6 {This is the Semi-Slav Defense, Accelerated Move Order.}
4. cxd5 {Fixes the pawns before Black can choose.} exd5 {Six players in ten take back with the e-pawn, and the Carlsbad structure appears.}
(4... cxd5 {A symmetrical structure.} 5. Bf4 {[%cal Gf4b8] The bishop takes the open diagonal.} Nc6 6. e3 Nf6 7. Bd3 {Next Nf3 or Nge2, then castle. White's bishops are more active. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.})
5. Bf4 {[%cal Gf4b8] With no knight on f6 to pin, the bishop goes to f4. Against 5...Bd6, trade with 6.Bxd6 Qxd6 7.e3 (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} Nf6
6. e3 {Next Bd3, Nf3 or Nge2, castle, and the minority attack, as in the Exchange. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "QGD: 3...Bb4"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Queen's Knight Variation"]
[ECO "D31"]

{3...Bb4 pins the knight at once, in about one game in eleven. Take on d5, as in the Exchange. After 4...exd5 5.Nf3 Nf6 6.Bg5 the game looks like the Exchange Variation, with Black's bishop on b4.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4
4. cxd5 {Two other recaptures are common. After 4...Bxc3+ 5.bxc3 exd5, 6.Ba3 keeps Black's king from castling for now, and White has the bishop pair (Stockfish +0.7, slightly better for White). After 4...Qxd5, 5.e3 and Nf3 develop, and the queen will be chased (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White).} exd5
5. Nf3 Nf6 6. Bg5 {The same pin as in the Exchange.} h6 7. Bxf6 Qxf6 8. e3 c6
9. Bd3 {Castle next, then the minority attack or e4. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "QGD: 3...c5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Tarrasch Defense: Classical Variation, Carlsbad Variation"]
[ECO "D34"]

{In about one game in twenty after 3.Nc3, Black plays 3...c5 and accepts an isolated pawn on d5 in return for free piece play. Take on d5, then play g3 and Bg2: the bishop on the long diagonal hits d5, the weak pawn. Against 4...cxd4, take the pawn and keep it.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 c5 {This is the Tarrasch Defense.}
4. cxd5 exd5
(4... cxd4 {This is the Schara Gambit. Black gives a pawn for quick development.} 5. Qxd4 Nc6 6. Qd1 {The queen goes home, out of reach of Black's pieces.} exd5 7. Qxd5 {White is a pawn up. Black has some development in return, but not enough. Stockfish: +0.5, slightly better for White.})
5. Nf3 Nc6 6. g3 {[%cal Gf1g2,Gg2d5] This is the Rubinstein System. The bishop goes to g2, where it hits d5.} Nf6 {This is the Prague Variation.}
7. Bg2 Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. Bg5 {[%cal Gg5f6,Rf6d5] This is the Carlsbad Variation. The bishop hits f6, a defender of d5. White aims to trade on f6, then win d5 or blockade it. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Slav: 5...cxd5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Semi-Slav Defense"]
[ECO "D43"]

{About three Semi-Slav players in ten take back on d5 with the c-pawn. The pawns are symmetrical, but Black's c8 bishop is shut in behind e6 and White's bishops are free. Develop with Bg5, e3 and Bd3, and use the open c-file.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. cxd5 cxd5
6. Bg5 {[%cal Gg5f6] Pins the knight.} Be7 7. e3 O-O 8. Bd3 Nc6
9. Rc1 {[%cal Gc1c8] The rook takes the open c-file. Castle next. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Slav: ...Bg4 and Ne5"]
[Diagram "4...Bg4"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Three Knights Variation"]
[ECO "D15"]

{Against an early ...Bg4, jump into e5 with the knight, which hits the bishop. Most Black players then go to h5 or f5, and both moves lose ground to cxd5 and Qb3, because b7 has no guard. Black's best is to go back to c8, with a slightly worse game.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6
(3... Bg4 4. Ne5 {Hits the bishop. 4...Bc8 is Black's best (Stockfish +0.8). After 4...Bf5?, 5.cxd5 Bxb1 6.dxc6 Nxc6 7.Nxc6 bxc6 8.Rxb1 leaves Black's pawns weak (Stockfish +2.6).} Bh5? {The most common reply, but the bishop leaves the queenside.} 5. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7] b7 has no guard, and the bishop on h5 can't help.} Qc8 6. cxd5 cxd5 7. Nc3 {Black is behind in development, and d5 is under fire. Stockfish: +2.7, winning for White.})
4. Nc3 Bg4 5. Ne5 {[%cal Ge5g4] Hits the bishop.

Black's best is 5...Bc8 (Stockfish +0.8, slightly better for White). After 5...Bf5?, 6.cxd5 and e4 gain time (Stockfish +2.6, winning for White). After 5...e6, 6.f3 chases the bishop with g4 and h4 (Stockfish +3.0, winning for White).} Bh5? {The most common reply, but the bishop leaves the queenside.}
6. cxd5 cxd5 7. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7,Gb3d5] Hits b7 and adds pressure on d5. Stockfish: +3.3, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Slav: 4...dxc4"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Czech Variation, Classical System, Main Line"]
[ECO "D19"]

{About one Slav player in nine takes on c4 after 4.Nc3. Play 5.a4 to stop ...b5, which would hold the pawn. White wins the pawn back with e3 and Bxc4, castles, and prepares e4 with Qe2.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 dxc4
5. a4 {[%cal Ga4b5] This is the Alapin Variation. It stops ...b5. Against 5...e6, play 6.e3 c5 7.Bxc4 (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).} Bf5 {This is the Czech Variation. Black develops the bishop before ...e6.}
6. e3 {Opens the diagonal of the f1 bishop.} e6 7. Bxc4 {The pawn is back.} Bb4 {[%cal Rb4c3] Pins the knight, so e4 is harder to play.}
8. O-O {This is the Dutch Variation.} O-O 9. Qe2 {[%cal Ge3e4,Gf1d1] Prepares e4 and Rd1. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Slav: 4...g6"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Schlechter Variation"]
[ECO "D15"]

{4...g6 comes up in about one Slav game in thirteen, and 4...a6, the Chebanenko Variation, in about one in twenty. Against 4...g6, take on d5 and put the bishop on f4, the diagonal that Black's fianchetto leaves alone. Against 4...a6, play 5.a4, which stops ...b5, and develop normally (Stockfish +0.2, equal).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 g6 {This is the Schlechter Variation. Black prepares ...Bg7.}
5. cxd5 {Against 5...Nxd5, 6.e4 takes the full center (Stockfish +1.0, clearly better for White).} cxd5
6. Bf4 {[%cal Gf4b8] The bishop takes the open diagonal.} Nc6 7. e3 {Next Bd3 or Be2, castle, and Rc1. Stockfish: +0.4, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Slav: 3...dxc4"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Modern Line"]
[ECO "D11"]

{About one Slav player in twenty takes on c4 at once, and two in three of them try to keep the pawn with 4...b5. Then 5.a4 hits the chain, and b3 breaks it, as in the Accepted.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 dxc4 4. e3 {Bxc4 is coming.} b5
5. a4 {[%cal Ga4b5] Hits the base of the chain.} e6 6. axb5 cxb5 7. b3 {[%cal Gb3c4] Hits the head of the chain.} Nf6 8. bxc4 bxc4
9. Bxc4 {White has the pawn back and the better center. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Slav: 3...Bf5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Modern Line"]
[ECO "D11"]

{About one Slav player in eight brings the bishop out on move 3. Now only the queen guards d5, and nothing guards b7. Take on d5 and play Qb3, which hits both. Every Black reply leaves White better, and the most common ones lose material.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Bf5
4. cxd5 {4...Qxd5 brings the queen out early: 5.Nc3 hits it (Stockfish +2.0, winning for White).} cxd5
5. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7,Gb3d5] Hits b7 and d5.

5...b6? and 5...Qd7? lose material, as in the trap chapter on ...b6? and ...Qd7?: 5...b6 6.e4! (Stockfish +4.1), or 5...Qd7 6.Ne5 Qc8 7.Nc3 (Stockfish +3.8). 5...Qb6 6.Qxd5 e6 7.Qb3 Qxb3 8.axb3 leaves White a pawn up in the endgame (Stockfish +1.3). Black's best is 5...Nf6, which gives up b7 for development: 6.Qxb7 Nbd7 7.Nc3 (Stockfish +0.9, slightly better for White).} Qc7? {Guards b7, but not d5.}
6. Nc3 {[%cal Gc3d5,Gc3b5] Hits d5 again, and Nb5 is coming.} Nf6 7. e4! {Opens lines while Black's king is in the center.} dxe4 8. Ne5 e6
9. Bf4 {[%cal Gf4c7] Hits the queen. Bb5+ comes next. Stockfish: +2.4, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Accepted: 3...Bf5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted: Old Variation"]
[ECO "D20"]

{About one Accepted player in eleven develops the bishop with 3...Bf5. White takes back on c4 and hits b7 with Qb3, as against the Slav. Most players then weaken their queenside with ...b6.

3...c5 is rarer. Take back with 4.Bxc4, and after 4...cxd4 5.exd4, develop with Nf3, Nc3 and castle: the isolated pawn gives White active play (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 Bf5
4. Bxc4 {4...Bxb1?! 5.Rxb1 gives White the bishop pair (Stockfish +1.7, clearly better for White).} e6
5. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7,Gc4e6] Hits b7, and the bishop on c4 eyes e6.} b6 {Six players in ten guard b7 this way. 5...Qc8 is the other main move.}
6. Nc3 Nf6 7. Nf3 {Castle next. White is ahead in development, and Black's queenside is loose. Stockfish: +0.9, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Marshall: other fourth moves"]
[Diagram "4...Bg4"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Marshall Defense"]
[ECO "D06"]

{After 3...Nxd5 4.Nf3, Black has three more ideas, each played in one game in six to eight: develop the bishop with 4...Bg4 or 4...Bf5, or fianchetto with 4...g6. White keeps more space and a clear edge in all of them. Against 4...Bf5, play 5.Nbd2, ready for Qb3 and e4 (Stockfish +0.9).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 Nf6 3. cxd5 Nxd5 4. Nf3 Bg4
(4... g6 5. e4 Nb6 {5...Nf6 6.Nc3 Bg7 is similar (Stockfish +1.7, clearly better for White).} 6. h3 {Keeps Black's bishop off g4.} Bg7 7. Nc3 O-O 8. Be3 {[%cal Gd1d2,Ge3h6] White has the full center and plans Qd2 and Bh6. Stockfish: +1.2, clearly better for White.})
5. Ne5 {[%cal Ge5g4] Hits the bishop.} Bh5 6. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7,Gb3d5] Hits b7 and the knight on d5.} Nb6
7. g4 {[%cal Gg4h5] Chases the bishop.} Bg6 8. Bg2 {[%cal Gg2b7] The bishop joins the pressure on b7. Next Nxg6 wins the bishop pair. Stockfish: +1.5, clearly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Marshall: 3...Qxd5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Marshall Defense"]
[ECO "D06"]

{About one Marshall player in five takes back with the queen. 4.Nc3 gains time on it. Most players retreat to d8, and then e4 builds the full center. After 4...Qa5, watch the queen: with a bishop on d2, any knight move uncovers an attack on it.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 Nf6 3. cxd5 Qxd5 4. Nc3 {Gains time on the queen.} Qd8
(4... Qa5 5. Nf3 Bg4 {The most common reply. 5...Nc6? 6.Bd2 Bg4 7.e4! Qb6 8.d5 is worse for Black (Stockfish +3.0, winning for White).} 6. Bd2 {[%cal Gd2a5] Now any move of the c3 knight uncovers an attack on the queen.} Bxf3 7. gxf3 e6 8. e4 {White has the bishop pair and the center. Stockfish: +1.5, clearly better for White.})
5. Nf3 e6 6. e4 {[%csl Yd4,Ye4] The full center.} c5 7. d5 {Gains more space.} exd5
8. exd5 {[%cal Gd5d6] The passed pawn on d5 cramps Black. Bd3 and castling come next. Stockfish: +1.2, clearly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Other second moves"]
[Diagram "2...Bf5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Baltic Defense"]
[ECO "D06"]

{Three rare second moves, each in about one game in thirty to sixty. Against each one, take on d5 or develop: Black has given up the center, and White's pieces come out with gain of time.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 Bf5 {This is the Baltic Defense. The bishop comes out before ...e6.}
(2... c5 {This is the Austrian Defense.} 3. cxd5 Qxd5 4. Nf3 cxd4 5. Nc3 Qa5 6. Nxd4 Nf6 7. Nb3 {[%cal Gb3a5] Hits the queen again. White is ahead in development. Stockfish: +0.5, slightly better for White.})
(2... Nc6 {This is the Chigorin Defense. The knight blocks the c-pawn.} 3. Nf3 e6 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. Bg5 Be7 6. e3 {A Queen's Gambit Declined in which Black's knight on c6 blocks ...c5 and ...c6, Black's usual freeing moves. Stockfish: +0.5, slightly better for White.})
3. cxd5 Qxd5? {The most common reply, but the queen gets chased.}
(3... Bxb1 {Black gives up the bishop to win d5 back safely.} 4. Rxb1 Qxd5 5. e3 {Develop with Nf3, Be2 or Bd3, and castle. Black has given up the bishop pair. Stockfish: +1.0, clearly better for White.})
4. Nc3 {Hits the queen.} Qd8 5. e4 {[%cal Ge4f5] Gains more time on the bishop.} Bg6 6. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7] Hits b7.} e6
7. Qxb7 {White is a pawn up and ahead in development. Stockfish: +2.5, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 3...b5 and 4.a4"]
[Diagram "3...b5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted: Old Variation"]
[ECO "D20"]

{Almost one Accepted player in five tries to keep the pawn with 3...b5. It doesn't work. 4.a4 attacks the chain at its base, and the long diagonal from f3 to a8 becomes a weapon. Black's best is 4...b4, which gives the pawn back for a slightly worse game. The natural 4...c6 and 4...a6 lose material.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 b5?! {Holds the pawn on c4, but the pawns on b5 and c4 can be attacked.}
4. a4 {[%cal Ga4b5] Hits b5, the base of the chain. 4...bxa4 5.Qxa4+ checks and wins the c4 pawn next (Stockfish +1.0, clearly better for White).} c6?! {The natural way to hold b5, and the most common.}
(4... b4 {The best defense: Black gives the pawn back and keeps the queenside closed.} 5. Bxc4 {White has the pawn back and a free hand in the center. Stockfish: +0.9, slightly better for White.})
(4... a6? 5. axb5 axb5?? {Opens the a-file. 5...Bb7 was better, though White stays clearly better (Stockfish +1.0).} 6. Rxa8 {The rook on a8 had no defender. Stockfish: +7.0, winning for White.})
5. axb5 {Takes the base of the chain.} cxb5?? {Holds c4, but opens the long diagonal to the rook on a8. 5...Nf6 was the best try, and White is still clearly better (Stockfish +1.9).}
6. Qf3 {[%cal Gf3a8] Hits the rook on a8. Black has to give up a piece to save it.} Nc6 {The best try: the knight blocks the diagonal.}
7. Qxc6+ Bd7 8. Qa6 {[%cal Ga6b5] White is a piece up, and the b5 pawn is next. Stockfish: +5.7, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 5...Bg4? and Qb3"]
[Diagram "5...Bg4"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted: Old Variation"]
[ECO "D20"]

{When Black's light-squared bishop leaves c8, b7 is loose. A queen on b3 then hits b7, and together with the bishop on c4 it hits f7 too. In this line, grabbing the knight on f3 even allows a mate in two.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 Nf6 4. Bxc4 Nc6 {Develops, but blocks the c-pawn, Black's main freeing break. 4...Bg4?! at once runs into the same 5.Qb3 (Stockfish +1.3, clearly better for White).}
5. Nf3 Bg4? {Pins the knight, but b7 is now loose.}
6. Qb3! {[%cal Gb3b7,Gc4f7] Hits b7 and f7.} Bxf3?? {Takes a piece, but f7 falls with check.}
(6... e6 {Black gives up b7.} 7. Qxb7 {White is a pawn up, and the knight on c6 is attacked. Stockfish: +1.9, clearly better for White.})
(6... Na5? {Hits the queen and the bishop at once.} 7. Bxf7+! {The bishop escapes with check.} Kd7 8. Ne5+ {Another check, and the queen still has time to move.} Kc8 9. Qa4 {[%cal Ga4a5] The queen steps away and hits the knight on a5.} Bf5 10. Qxa5 {White is a knight and a pawn up, and Black's king is exposed. Stockfish: +8.0, winning for White.})
7. Bxf7+ Kd7 8. Qe6# *

[Chapter "Trap: 7...Nh5? and Nxd5"]
[Diagram "7...Nh5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation, Reshevsky Variation"]
[ECO "D36"]

{In the Exchange Variation, Black often tries to trade off White's strong bishop with ...Nh5. Played before Black's knight has left b8, it loses a pawn: nothing guards c8, so the c6 pawn can't safely take back on d5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 c6 6. Qc2 Be7 7. e3 Nh5? {Gets out of the e7 bishop's way, so ...Bxg5 is threatened.}
8. Bxe7 Qxe7
9. Nxd5! {[%cal Gc2c8] The c6 pawn can't take back safely: the c-file would open, and the queen on c2 takes the bishop on c8.} cxd5?
(9... Qd6 {The best defense: the queen hits the knight.} 10. Nc3 {The knight goes back. Black is a pawn down, and the knight on h5 is far from the center. Stockfish: +3.1, winning for White.})
10. Qxc8+ Qd8 11. Bb5+ Nc6 12. Bxc6+ bxc6 13. Qxc6+ {White is two pawns up, and the queen attacks the rook on a8 too. Stockfish: +5.5, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Trap: ...b6? and ...Qd7? against Qb3"]
[Diagram "6...b6"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Three Knights Variation"]
[ECO "D15"]

{In the Slav with an early ...Bf5, 6.Qb3 hits b7. Many players guard it with 6...b6 or 6...Qd7. Both lose to 7.e4!: the pawn opens the e-file and the diagonal from f1 to a6, and the knight jumps to e5 with gain of time. Black's king is stuck in the center.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 Bf5 5. cxd5 cxd5 6. Qb3 b6? {Guards b7, but Black is behind in development and the king is still on e8.}
(6... Qd7? 7. e4! Nxe4 8. Ne5 {Hits the queen.} Qc8 9. g4 {[%cal Gg4f5] The bishop on f5 is trapped. White wins a piece for a pawn. Stockfish: +4.3, winning for White.})
7. e4! {Opens lines while Black's king is still in the center.} dxe4
8. Ne5 {[%cal Ge5f7,Gf1b5] The knight eyes f7 and d7, and Bb5+ comes next.} Be6 9. Bb5+ Nfd7
10. d5 {Drives the bishop away from e6. Black's pieces are tied up and the king can't castle. Stockfish: +3.8, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Trap: the Greek gift"]
[Diagram "7...O-O"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Marshall Defense"]
[ECO "D06"]

{In the Marshall Defense, almost half of all Black players castle on move 7. That is a blunder: 8.e5 drives the knight from f6, Black's best defender of the king, and the classic bishop sacrifice on h7 follows. This sacrifice is known as the Greek gift.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 Nf6 3. cxd5 Nxd5 4. Nf3 e6 5. e4 Nf6 6. Nc3 Bb4 7. Bd3 O-O?? {7...Nc6 or 7...c5 was necessary: see the Marshall chapter.}
8. e5! {[%cal Ge5f6] Drives the knight away from f6. After 8...Ng4, the best try, 9.h3 Nh6 10.Bxh6 gxh6 leaves Black's king wide open (Stockfish +4.6, winning for White).} Nd5
9. Bxh7+! {[%cal Gd3h7] The Greek gift.} Kxh7 10. Ng5+ {[%cal Gd1h5] The knight checks and makes room for the queen. After 10...Kg6, 11.h4 brings another attacker (Stockfish +7.9, winning for White).} Kg8
11. Qh5 {[%cal Gh5h7] Threatens Qh7#. Black can only stop it by giving up the queen: 11...Re8? 12.Qxf7+ leads to mate.} Qxg5 12. Bxg5 {White has won the queen for a bishop and a knight. Stockfish: +7.3, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 7...Ne5? and Nxe5"]
[Diagram "7...Ne5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Marshall Defense"]
[ECO "D06"]

{In the Marshall Defense with 4...Nc6, after 5.e4 Nf6 6.Nc3 Bg4 7.d5, Black is already in trouble. Three Black players in four now play 7...Ne5?, which offers White's queen for a knight. Take it: 8.Nxe5! gives up the queen, and two bishop checks win it back with interest.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 Nf6 3. cxd5 Nxd5 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. e4 Nf6 6. Nc3 Bg4
7. d5 {[%cal Gd5c6] Hits the knight on c6.} Ne5?
(7... Bxf3 {Black gives up the bishop to keep the knight.} 8. gxf3 Nb8 9. e5 {White has the center and Black's pieces are driven back. Stockfish: +4.5, winning for White.})
8. Nxe5! {[%cal Gf1b5] Takes the knight and leaves the queen hanging.} Bxd1 9. Bb5+ {The check forces Black to block.} c6 10. dxc6 {[%cal Gc6b7] Threatens cxb7+ with a discovered check.} a6 11. c7+ {Discovered check from the bishop on b5. Black can't save the queen on d8.} axb5 12. cxd8=Q+ Rxd8 13. Nxd1 {White has won back the queen and is a knight up. Stockfish: +5.9, winning for White.} *
